.TH TRACKER 1 "August 2026" "tracker 1.0.0" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
tracker \- simple project tracker by MattFor

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tracker
.RI "<command> " "[arguments] [options]"
.br
.B tracker
.RI "<project> <command> " "[arguments] [options]"

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tracker
tracks projects by discovering Git repositories and storing project metadata
such as a status, a free form note and when the project was last touched.

Running
.B tracker
with no command lists the tracked projects.

Commands that act on a project accept the project either after the command or
before it, so
.B "tracker edit 12 status active"
and
.B "tracker 12 edit status active"
are the same.

.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR help ", " h ", " -h
Show help.

.TP
.BR version ", " v ", " -v
Show the program version.

.TP
.BR list ", " l ", " ls
List tracked projects. Accepts a search word, a limit, a
.B regex
pattern, list filters and temporary setting overrides in
.IR setting = value
form.

.TP
.BR check ", " c ", " cc ", " info
Show everything known about a project: its tracking data, where it lives, how
much space it uses, its Git branch, remote, last commit and working tree state,
the language and version read from its build files and its note.

.TP
.BR add ", " a
Add a project, or scan a directory and add the Git projects below it.

.TP
.BR remove ", " rm ", " r
Remove tracked projects. Nothing is deleted from disk. Removing more than one
project at a time asks for confirmation unless
.B --yes
is given.

.TP
.BR edit ", " e
Change the
.B status
or
.B note
of the selected projects. The field names may be shortened to
.BR s ", " st " for status and " n ", " nt " for note."

.TP
.BR note ", " n
Set the note of the selected projects without naming the field, for example
.BR "tracker note 12 review sunday" .

.TP
.BR status ", " st
Set the status of the selected projects without naming the field, for example
.BR "tracker 12 st blocked" .

.TP
.BR forget ", " ignore
Stop tracking the selected projects and keep future scans from finding them
again. The paths are written to
.B scan.exclude
in the settings file, which a running daemon picks up on its next scan. Takes
.BR list ", " clear " and " "clear <pattern>" .

.TP
.BR init ", " i ", " scan
Scan a directory and merge what it finds into the database. Existing entries
keep their ID, status and note.

.TP
.BR path ", " p ", " where
Print the path of a project, for example
.BR "cd \(dq$(tracker path tracker)\(dq" .

.TP
.BR stats ", " summary
Show totals, a breakdown by status and the most and least recently touched
projects.

.TP
.B completion
Print a shell completion script for
.BR bash ", " zsh " or " fish ,
completing command names and tracked project names for both
.B tracker
and
.BR t .
.PP
.nf
eval "$(tracker completion bash)"
tracker completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/tracker.fish
.fi

.TP
.BR settings ", " s ", " config
View or change the configuration. Takes
.BR edit ", " path ", " get " and " set .

.TP
.BR daemon ", " d ", " bg
Control the background scanner. Takes
.BR start ", " stop ", " restart ", " status ", " log " and " run .

.SH SHORT FORMS
A command and one of its own actions may be written as a single word, so
.B tracker dst
is
.BR "tracker daemon status" .

.TP
.BR ds ", " dst ", " dk ", " dr ", " dl
daemon start, status, stop, restart, log
.TP
.BR sg ", " ss ", " se ", " sp
settings get, set, edit, path
.TP
.BR fl ", " fc
forget list, forget clear
.PP
Only a command and its own action fuse.
.BR -- .

.SH SELECTION
.TP
.B ID
Permanent global ID.
.TP
.B TID
Temporary ID from the current sorted view.
.TP
.BI i: n
Force the permanent ID.
.B id:
is accepted too.
.TP
.BI t: n
Force the temporary ID.
.B tid:
is accepted too.
.TP
.BI # n
Force the permanent ID. Shells treat a bare
.B #5
as a comment, so quote it or use
.B id:5
instead.
.TP
.BI @ n
Force the temporary ID.
.TP
.IB n - x
Inclusive range, in either direction.
.TP
.IB n + x
Project n and the next x projects.
.TP
.B all
Every tracked project. Prefer this to
.BR * ,
which the shell expands to a directory listing before tracker sees it.
.TP
.B name
Exact or partial project name.
.TP
.B path
Exact or partial project path. A path that exists on disk is resolved first, so
tab completed and relative paths work.
.PP
.B remove
and
.B forget
do nothing at all unless every selector resolved.

.SH FILTERS
.TP
.BI +m: value
Include partial name or path matches.
.TP
.BI -m: value
Exclude partial name or path matches.
.TP
.BI +r: regex
Include names matching a regex.
.TP
.BI -r: regex
Exclude names matching a regex.

.SH ROW FORMAT
.B display.format
lays each row out by hand instead of using
.B display.columns
and the separators. The known fields are
.BR $tid ", " $id ", " $name ", " $path ", " $status ", " $last_touched ,
.BR $version ", " $language " and " $note .

Fields are padded so the output stays aligned.
.B $>
aligns a field to the right and
.BI ${ name }
lets text follow a field immediately. Anything that is not a known field is
printed as written.

A format containing
.B $note
places the note itself, otherwise the usual inline or below placement is used.

Quote a format with single quotes; a shell expands
.B $name
inside double quotes.
.PP
.nf
tracker list 'display.format=$>tid. $name [$status]'
tracker settings set display.format '${tid}) $name - $note'
.fi

.SH NOTES
A note is printed on the same line as its project and only drops to a line of
its own when the terminal is too narrow. This is controlled by
.BR display.note_position ,
.B display.note_min_width
and
.BR display.show_notes .

.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR verbose ", " vv ", " --verbose
Explain what is happening while it happens.
.TP
.BR --yes ", " -y ", " --force
Do not ask for confirmation.
.TP
.B --
Treat everything after it as text rather than options or commands, for a note
that starts with a dash or contains a command name.
.TP
.BR --no-colour ", " --colour
Force colour off or on.

.SH CONFIGURATION
Tracker loads
.I ~/.config/tracker/settings.toml
and falls back to the defaults shipped with the package.
.B "tracker settings edit"
and
.B "tracker settings set"
create that file from the shipped copy the first time they are used; nothing is
ever written inside the installation prefix.

Run from a source checkout it loads
.I my_settings.toml
and then
.I settings.toml
from the checkout instead, and keeps the database there too.

Any setting left out of your file falls back to the built in default, so a
partial configuration file is fine.

Settings can be overridden for a single command:
.PP
.nf
tracker list display.list_limit=20
tracker list sorting.by=name output.compact=true
.fi

.SH DAEMON
The daemon periodically scans the directories configured under
.B [daemon]
in the settings file, adding new projects and archiving ones that disappeared.
It runs detached and writes to a log file.

It also re-reads the settings file whenever it changes, so
.B scan.exclude
and the watched paths take effect without a restart.

.TP
.B tracker daemon
Start the daemon.
.TP
.B tracker daemon stop
Stop the running daemon.
.TP
.B tracker daemon status
Show whether it is running, where its log is and what it watches.
.TP
.B tracker daemon log
Show the end of the daemon log.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TRACKER_SETTINGS
Use a different settings file.
.TP
.B TRACKER_DATA
Use a different database file.
.TP
.B TRACKER_SHARE
Look for the shipped help text and default settings in this directory.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
Disable colour.

.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
tracker
tracker list
tracker list tracker
tracker list 20
tracker list -m:archived
tracker add ~/Programming/Projects active "work in progress"
tracker check tracker
tracker edit 3-7 status completed
tracker id:12 note review sunday
tracker tracker st current
tracker remove tracker
tracker init ~/Programming
tracker stats
tracker settings set display.list_limit 30
tracker daemon status
.fi

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tracker/settings.toml
Your configuration.

.TP
.I ~/.local/share/tracker/data.pkl
Project database.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/daemon.log
Daemon log.

.TP
.I ~/.local/state/tracker/daemon.pid
Daemon process ID.

.SH AUTHOR
MattFor

.SH VERSION
1.0.0
